  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nEarlier this morning, one upper level trough was located over \nthe Upper Mississippi Valley while further west our next upper \nlow was starting to move across California. Between these two \nsystems was an upper level ridge and as this upper ridge crosses\nthe Central Plains over the next 24 to 36 hours a period of dry\nconditions along with more seasonal temperatures can be \nexpected across southwest Kansas Today (Tuesday) and Tuesday \nnight. \n\nOn Wednesday this upper ridge will begin to move east of the \narea which will allow for a southwesterly flow aloft to develop \nas the California upper low moves east towards the four corners \nregion. As this occurs the surface pressures will begin to fall \nalong the lee of the Rockies and result in increasing southerly \nlow level winds. This will bring improving gulf moisture return \naround the western edge of the upper ridge and into southwest \nkansas based on the latest IVT mid week and increasing PWAT \nvalues. By late Wednesday the PWATs across western Kansas is \nforecast to be greater than the 95th percentile. The combination\nof the enhanced lift developing ahead of the approaching upper \nlevel trough and improving moisture will result in increasing \nchances for widespread thunderstorms from Wednesday night \nthrough Friday. These storms mid to late week will be capable of\nproducing locally heavy rainfall given the high PWATs and \nimproving lift. The risk of severe weather late week at this \ntime appears low given the forecast instability and shear.\n\nRainfall accumulations late week. All of the ensemble clusters \ncontinue to place the the higher 24 hour rainfall totals ending\nat 7pm Thursday west of a Hays to Dodge City to Liberal line \nwith greater than a 60% chance for 24 hour rainfall totals to be\ngreater than 1/2 inch. The best chances for the higher rainfall\ntotals will be west of highway 83 where there is a 80 to near \n95 percent chance for rainfall totals greater than 1/2 inch. \nBased on this latest rainfall forecast there is currently a \nmarginal risk for excessive rainfall for areas west of highway \n183 and there is a slight risk for excessive rainfall across \nextreme southwest Kansas. In the slight risk area the risk of \nexcessive rainfall Wednesday night there is currently a greater \nthan 60% chance for greater that 1 inch will be possible from \nWednesday afternoon through Thursday afternoon. Those with \noutdoor plains mid to late week should monitor this closely. \n\nThe area of widespread accumulating rainfall across far \nsouthwest Kansas Wednesday night will shift east towards \nCentral Kansas Thursday night and Friday as approaching upper \nlevel trough exits the Rockies and moves out into the Plains and\na surface boundary in eastern Colorado moves east/northeast \nacross southwest Kansas. Areas of steadier and heavier rainfall \nwill continue ahead of the surface boundary which by late \nThursday night/early Friday is forecast to be located mainly \neast of Highway 283.\n\nOver the weekend an upper ridge will build across the Rockies \nand out into the plains as the upper trough continues to move\neast/northeast and away from western Kansas which will result \nin weekend warmup and decreasing precipitation chances across \nsouthwest Kansas. Highs in the upper 80s to near 95 degrees will\nbe possible on Saturday and Sunday.",
